# Group 4 input triage rail (shadow-first) + the per-call dispatcher that keeps it
# mutually exclusive with the enforcing `self check input` rail.
#
# WHY A DISPATCHER: in this NeMo version `run input rails` iterates the FULL
# config.rails.input.flows list on EVERY input-rail generate() — the per-request
# GenerationOptions.rails.input list only toggles input rails on/off, it does NOT
# select WHICH input flows run (unlike the output lane). Co-listing both rails
# would therefore run BOTH on every call: the triage call would also fire the
# enforcing self-check (wasted call + a stray InputRailException corrupting the
# triage read), and the self-check call would also fire triage. So a SINGLE
# dispatcher flow is listed, and the pod passes a `$triage_mode` context variable
# per call to route to exactly ONE lane — the enforcing self-check is byte-for-
# byte unchanged (same built-in flow, same action, same InputRailException), and
# the triage runs INDEPENDENTLY in its own generate() call.
#
# With `enable_rails_exceptions: true`, `create event <Name>Exception` surfaces as
# a `role: "exception"` turn whose content `type` is the event name; the two
# blocking triage labels use TWO DISTINCT exception types so the orchestrator can
# tell an attack BLOCK from a topic REDIRECT. An `ok` verdict creates no exception.

define flow guarded input
  if $triage_mode
    do input triage
  else
    do self check input

define flow input triage
  $verdict = execute input_triage_check
  if $verdict == "attack"
    create event TriageAttackException(message="input triage: attack")
    stop
  if $verdict == "offtopic"
    create event TriageRedirectException(message="input triage: offtopic")
    stop
